Let \(r_{o1}\) and \(r_{o2}\) be the small-signal output resistances of \(M_1\) and \(M_2\). Neglecting body effect:

- \(V_{DD}\) and the gate biases are AC grounds.
- Since the gate and source of \(M_2\) have no small-signal voltage, \(M_2\) appears at node \(X\) as simply \(r_{o2}\) to ground.
- Thus, \(M_1\) is source-degenerated by \(r_{o2}\).

Therefore,
\[
\boxed{
R_{\text{out}}
=r_{o1}+\left(1+g_{m1}r_{o1}\right)r_{o2}
=r_{o1}+r_{o2}+g_{m1}r_{o1}r_{o2}
}
\]

Typically, \(g_{m1}r_{o1}\gg 1\), so
\[
\boxed{R_{\text{out}}\approx g_{m1}r_{o1}r_{o2}.}
\]

If the body effect of \(M_1\) is included, replace \(g_{m1}\) with \(g_{m1}+g_{mb1}\).

The bias voltages and \(V_{DD}\) are small-signal grounds. Since \(M_2\) has both its gate and source at ac ground, its controlled current source is zero; thus, looking into node \(X\), \(M_2\) presents only \(r_{O2}\).

Therefore, \(M_1\) has source degeneration \(r_{O2}\), giving

\[
\boxed{
R_{\text{out}}
=r_{O1}+\left(1+g_{m1}r_{O1}\right)r_{O2}
=r_{O1}+r_{O2}+g_{m1}r_{O1}r_{O2}
}
\]

where body effect has been neglected. Usually \(g_{m1}r_{O1}r_{O2}\) dominates, so

\[
R_{\text{out}}\approx g_{m1}r_{O1}r_{O2}.
\]

If the body effect of \(M_1\) is included, replace \(g_{m1}\) by \(g_{m1}+g_{mb1}\) in the product term.

For small signals, \(V_{DD}\), \(V_{b1}\), and \(V_{b2}\) are AC grounds. Thus, \(M_2\) presents a resistance \(r_{o2}\) from node \(X\) to ground, which acts as source degeneration for \(M_1\).

Including the body effect of \(M_1\), the output resistance is

\[
\boxed{R_{\text{out}}
=r_{o1}+r_{o2}+\left(g_{m1}+g_{mb1}\right)r_{o1}r_{o2}}
\]

where \(r_{o1},r_{o2}\) are the transistor output resistances.

Neglecting body effect,

\[
\boxed{R_{\text{out}}
=r_{o1}+r_{o2}+g_{m1}r_{o1}r_{o2}
\approx g_{m1}r_{o1}r_{o2}}
\]

when \(g_{m1}r_{o1}\gg 1\).

Using the simplified model in Figure 14(b), apply a test voltage \(V_1\) at the input. Since the gate of \(M_1\) draws no current, the test current flows through \(R_F\) and \(R_{D2}\), which are in series between \(V_1\) and the controlled-source voltage \(-AV_1\). Thus,

\[
I_{\text{test}}
=\frac{V_1-(-AV_1)}{R_F+R_{D2}}
=\frac{(1+A)V_1}{R_F+R_{D2}} .
\]

Therefore, the low-frequency input resistance looking into the TIA is

\[
\boxed{R_{\text{in}}=\frac{R_F+R_{D2}}{1+A}}
\]

or, when \(R_{D2}\ll R_F\),

\[
R_{\text{in}}\approx \frac{R_F}{1+A}.
\]

Hence, shunt feedback reduces the input resistance by approximately the loop-gain factor \(1+A\).

If the shunt capacitance \(C_{\text{in}}\) is also included in the impedance seen by \(I_{\text{in}}\), then

\[
\boxed{
Z_{\text{in}}(s)
=R_{\text{in}}\parallel \frac{1}{sC_{\text{in}}}
=\frac{R_F+R_{D2}}
{1+A+sC_{\text{in}}(R_F+R_{D2})}
}.
\]

Using the simplified model in Figure 14(b), apply a test voltage \(V_1\) at the input. For the low-frequency calculation, \(C_{\text{in}}\) is open.

The amplifier input draws no current, so the test current \(I_t\) flows through \(R_F\) and \(R_{D2}\). Since the controlled source produces \(-AV_1\),

\[
I_t=\frac{V_1-(-AV_1)}{R_F+R_{D2}}
=\frac{(1+A)V_1}{R_F+R_{D2}} .
\]

Hence the input resistance is

\[
\boxed{R_{\text{in}}=\frac{V_1}{I_t}
=\frac{R_F+R_{D2}}{1+A}}
\]

where \(A\) is the magnitude of the amplifier’s open-circuit voltage gain. If \(R_{D2}\ll R_F\), this reduces to the familiar approximation \(R_{\text{in}}\approx R_F/(1+A)\).

Including the shunt input capacitance, the complete input impedance is

\[
\boxed{
Z_{\text{in}}(s)=
\left[sC_{\text{in}}+\frac{1+A}{R_F+R_{D2}}\right]^{-1}
=
\frac{R_F+R_{D2}}
{1+A+sC_{\text{in}}(R_F+R_{D2})}
}.
\]
